A||||12||fropuff||Changing rear brake pads||||5||
Z||000000||fropuff||06-07-2001||02:04 PM||||<BR>Hey guys.  Since I busted a wheel stud when putting my wheel back on, I've decided to change the rear brake pads while I'm at it.  I know how to remove and replace the broken stud, but I don't know how I go about changing the rear pads.  I already did a search, and I've heard different things on how to do it.  I have an 86 GT, which I think it matters.  Do I need to get the tool thing when I'm getting the studs?  Thanks a lot!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters rock!||||reg||5
Z||000001||DecadenceR||06-07-2001||02:09 PM||||I would suggest it.<BR>It's not a neccesity, but it does make the job a lot easier...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-07-2001||02:20 PM||||what tool?!<P> you mean the torx bit? (I cant remember - its a #50?!)<P>  you need weird tools for the '88 brakes, but not for the rest of them.<P>  or do you mean the thing to turn the piston back in?||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||06-07-2001||03:26 PM||||Well, I'm getting my information from:  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009814.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009814.html</A>   As you can see, there's a lot of confusion.  Thanks!!!||||reg||4
Z||000004||Wannabe||06-07-2001||04:38 PM||||The Torx bit for the '88 brakes is a T-55. It's the only one you WON'T find in a normal tool kit or in the tool aisle at your typical auto parts store. Check out a specialty tool shop for it and don't even think of trying to get away with using a T-50.<p>[This message has been edited by Wannabe (edited 06-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||DecadenceR||06-07-2001||04:42 PM||||On my old 90 eclipse the rear calipers were the same design - rotating center. I did the brakes with a pair of needne nose pliers. however it slipped off a lot, and I got cut a few times. The brake tool is definitely worth the couple of bucks it costs, especially if you usually do your own work.||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||06-07-2001||05:34 PM||||You can use the tool to rotate the piston into the caliper, but the method in the Chilton manual (removing the E-Brake lever and bolt and then using a C-Clamp) works best.<P>I believe Ogre has a section about this in the Cave.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||06-07-2001||08:54 PM||||All the caliper bolts are T-50 on 84-86 Fiero. <P>As Deceler8 said... The correct method of compressing the piston is in my cave. GM does not recomend rotating these pistons. This caliper is not built like most others.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fropuff||06-08-2001||07:11 PM||||I can't understand why these cars are so frickin stupid!!!  Maybe I'm a little angry, but these brakes are POSs.  Why can't they be normal?  Anyway, I couldn't get the brakes done.  I don't know what I'm supposed to do with the clamp thing.  I followed the directions, and still no go.  Does the caliper come all the way off the rotor?  What about those two little caliper bolts?  Do I still need to take those out?  I took the e-brake things off, and I see that screw that I'm supposed to slip the socket over, and I've clamped quite a bit, but I still can't get it.  I guess I don't understand directions.  I really don't want to take it to a mechanic.  The minimum would be around $150, and I don't have that.  Please help!  Thanks!||||reg||11
Z||000009||theogre||06-08-2001||07:47 PM||||Dude, it's not rocket science. Get the haynes or chiltons book. They cover all this and have pictures. the haynes book is less than $20us.<P>My illustration is shown the way it is because you often have to compress the piston some to get the caliper over the rust rim on the rotor. And yes, The E brake Arm has to come off the caliper...<P>The slide bolts have to come out to get the pads off. The whole mess comes off the bracket for that part. You need a T50 Torx to get the bolts out.<P>DO NOT EVER use petrolium greese on brake parts. You must use Silicone or synthetic grease made specifically for this work.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fropuff||06-08-2001||10:49 PM||||Thanks for clarifying.  I have a few questions still...1) Do the T50 torx bolts come out before I compress the piston, or do I compress the piston first?  2) How much do I compress the piston?  I got it quite a ways; do I do it until the caliper can come off the rotor?  I think that's about it.  I think it wasn't working right because I was using a sliding bar clamp and not a C-clamp.  I'll have to find one and give it a try.  Thanks again!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Jncomutt||06-09-2001||01:37 AM||||As to your question for #1, I'm probably speaking the obvious, but yea, you gotta remove the bolts to get the caliper off, then if you slide them inside the sleeve, they shouldn't be a problem stayin within the caliper.  And for #2, The piston will only compress so far.  On all the ones I've played with so far, its just slightly above being completly flush.  By the way, Rather than using the C-clamp, for $3 you can pick up a brake compressor tool which I've found to make the job easier.<P>And if anyone else has trouble with those damn springs (as my young hands do)  I found it ALOT easier if you compress them in a clamp or vise, then use 3 zip ties (it must be 3, 2 will work but they slip to one side and you get left with a curved spring) to hold it compressed, push the cable through, connect it, then cut the zip ties... Takes only 3 mins and shaves a 'lil off the Blood pressure...<P>Good Luck!<P>------------------<BR>17 year old Fierophile<BR>'86 Coupe 160k miles<BR>'84 Mint Beauty 51k and original tires!||||reg||1
Z||000012||fropuff||06-09-2001||12:19 PM||||Oh.  I looked in the dealer's service manual, and it says to do the same stuff as you guys do, but it doesn't say to take out the T50s.  I thought I had to, but since it didn't say, I didn't think I should.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Thanks.  I'll have to get one of them brake compressor tool things.  Thanks again!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
